---
// PixlRGB, PIXL's working space: the specification (version 1, 2026-10-03) from
// pixl-engine's docs/pixlrgb, with its two ICC profiles. The text and the
// profiles are licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 by their author, so this page says so.
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro'
import '../../../styles/engine.css'
import GamutChart from '../../../components/GamutChart.astro'
import { href } from '../../../lib/links'
import { breadcrumbs } from '../../../lib/seo'

const contact = 'mailto:hello@pixlfoundation.com?subject=PixlRGB'
const profile = (f: string) => `/pixlrgb/${f}`
const licence = 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/'

const primaries = [
  { name: 'Red', x: '0.7360', y: '0.2644' },
  { name: 'Green', x: '−0.3256', y: '1.3204' },
  { name: 'Blue', x: '0.1414', y: '−0.0105' },
  { name: 'White (D65)', x: '0.3127', y: '0.3290' }
]
const steps = [
  { bits: '8', sky: '1.38', skin: '1.39', neutral: '0.41', p3: '0.71 / 0.78' },
  { bits: '10', sky: '0.34', skin: '0.34', neutral: '0.10', p3: '0.18 / 0.19' },
  { bits: '12', sky: '0.086', skin: '0.086', neutral: '0.026', p3: '0.044 / 0.048' },
  { bits: '16', sky: '0.0054', skin: '0.0054', neutral: '0.0027', p3: '0.0028 / 0.0030' }
]
const matrices = [
  {
    title: 'Linear PixlRGB → CIE XYZ (D65 white at Y = 1)',
    m: [['0.9401642213', '−0.1665451578', '0.1768368635'], ['0.3377437773', '0.6753876729', '−0.0131314503'], ['−0.0005109588', '0.0026598121', '1.0869088975']]
  },
  {
    title: 'CIE XYZ → linear PixlRGB',
    m: [['0.9768075112', '0.2414872446', '−0.1560061742'], ['−0.4884438167', '1.3598071400', '0.0958968251'], ['0.0016544875', '−0.0032141070', '0.9197322996']]
  },
  {
    title: 'Linear PixlRGB → linear Rec.2020',
    m: [['1.4939378931', '−0.5267895126', '0.0328516195'], ['−0.0808443527', '1.2028264927', '−0.1219821400'], ['0.0016574785', '−0.0293187605', '1.0276612821']]
  },
  {
    title: 'Linear Rec.2020 → linear PixlRGB (every entry positive)',
    m: [['0.6856241563', '0.3006112504', '0.0137645934'], ['0.0461034038', '0.8540014684', '0.0998951279'], ['0.0002094925', '0.0238794711', '0.9759110364']]
  }
]
---

<Base
  site="engine"
  path="/pixlrgb/"
  title="PixlRGB · A working space that holds every colour"
  description="PixlRGB is PIXL's working space: linear RGB whose primaries hold every colour there is. The specification, the primaries, the matrices and the ICC profiles, under CC BY-SA 4.0."
  nav={[
    { label: 'Definition', to: '#definition' },
    { label: 'Chart', to: '#chart' },
    { label: 'Matrices', to: '#matrices' },
    { label: 'Profiles', to: '#profiles' },
  ]}
  cta={{ label: 'Download profiles', to: '#profiles' }}
  jsonLd={[breadcrumbs('engine', [{ name: 'PIXL Engine', path: '/' }, { name: 'PixlRGB', path: '/pixlrgb/' }])]}
>
  <section class="wrap spec-hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <span class="b-chip">Specification · version 1 · 2026-10-03</span>
    <h1 class="b-disp" id="hero-title">PixlRGB.<br />A space that holds<br />every colour.</h1>
    <p class="b-body lede">
      PixlRGB is the working space of PIXL: linear RGB light whose primaries hold every colour there is. A camera's
      colours, and the colours of every standard RGB space, are positive in it. It exists so that an edit sees every
      colour a photograph holds, and an output is made from it by a conversion PIXL states.
    </p>
    <div class="ctas">
      <a class="b-cta en" href="#profiles">Download the ICC profiles</a>
      <a class="b-cta sec" href="#definition">Read the definition</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap band spec" id="definition" aria-labelledby="def-title">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="head-stack">
        <span class="b-micro">Definition</span>
        <h2 class="b-disp h2" id="def-title">Four chromaticities are the definition.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="b-body">The primaries and the white are CIE 1931 xy chromaticities. Everything else on this page follows from them. Green and blue lie outside the chromaticity diagram, as the primaries of any space that holds every colour must.</p>
    </div>
    <table class="measured">
      <caption class="sr-only">PixlRGB's primaries and white, in CIE 1931 xy</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">x</th><th scope="col">y</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{primaries.map((p) => <tr><th scope="row">{p.name}</th><td class="b-mono">{p.x}</td><td class="b-mono">{p.y}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
    <ul class="spec-list b-body">
      <li><strong>Linear form.</strong> R, G and B are proportional to light, and (1, 1, 1) is white. Values above 1 are light above white, and are kept: in PIXL's master, 1.0 is 203 cd/m² (ITU-R BT.2408's graphics white), and the master is held in 32-bit floats.</li>
      <li><strong>Integer form.</strong> V = L<sup>1/γ</sup> for 0 ≤ L ≤ 1: a pure power with no linear segment, where γ is 2.2 as an ICC profile holds it (144179/65536 = 2.19999695). It is for standard-dynamic-range files, at 10 bits or more. Light above white has no integer form; content with headroom is stored as linear floats.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="b-body">A space this wide spends its codes on colours no picture holds, so an integer step is larger than in a display space. The largest step between neighbouring codes on a smooth ramp in the integer form, in ΔE2000:</p>
    <table class="measured">
      <caption class="sr-only">Largest code step in the integer form, in ΔE2000</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Bits</th><th scope="col">Sky</th><th scope="col">Skin</th><th scope="col">Neutral</th><th scope="col">Display P3's sky / skin</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{steps.map((r) => <tr><th scope="row">{r.bits}</th><td class="b-mono">{r.sky}</td><td class="b-mono">{r.skin}</td><td class="b-mono">{r.neutral}</td><td class="b-mono">{r.p3}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="b-body">At 8 bits a gradient bands visibly, so PIXL writes PixlRGB integer files at 10 bits or more.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap band" id="chart" aria-labelledby="chart-title">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="head-stack">
        <span class="b-micro">The triangle</span>
        <h2 class="b-disp h2" id="chart-title">Why the primaries are where they are.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="b-body">The owner of PIXL chose them on 2026-10-03 from candidates the pass that made PixlRGB computed and charted. Switch the standard spaces on and off to see what each one holds.</p>
    </div>
    <GamutChart />
    <ol class="spec-list b-body steps">
      <li><strong>The visible range</strong> is the convex hull, in CIE 1931 xy, of the chromaticity of every row of the CIE 1931 2° colour-matching functions (360–830 nm at 1 nm). Every mixture of spectral lights lies on a chord between two of them, and the purple line closes the hull.</li>
      <li><strong>The standard primaries</strong> join it: those of sRGB, Display P3, Adobe RGB and Rec.2020, as published. Display P3's red and Rec.2020's have x + y = 1, so Z = 0, which no light has; they lie 1.15e-4 and 3.9e-5 outside the visible range. Holding them makes every colour of every standard space positive in PixlRGB.</li>
      <li><strong>The triangle of least area</strong> that holds all of these, its area measured in CIE 1976 u′v′, where equal areas are closer to equally many colours to the eye. Every triple on a 1° grid was scored, and the best eight refined by Nelder and Mead's simplex. They agree on the least area to 1.5e-7 of it.</li>
      <li><strong>Rounded outward</strong> to four decimals: of the triangles whose six coordinates each lie between one ten-thousandth below and two above the minimum's own, the least in u′v′ that still holds every point.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="b-body">The result holds the visible range and the standard primaries with a margin of 4.5e-6 in xy. Of its area, 6.17% in u′v′ (27.2% in xy) is not a colour. One code step of the integer form at 8 bits moves a mid-saturated colour by 0.86 ΔE2000 on average (Rec.2020: 0.58), at 16 bits by 0.0033.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap band spec" id="camera" aria-labelledby="cam-title">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="head-stack">
        <span class="b-micro">What a camera records</span>
        <h2 class="b-disp h2" id="cam-title">A camera's colour is an estimate.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="b-body">A camera's colour is a 3 × 3 estimate, so it can place a real surface outside the visible range. PIXL keeps such a value as the camera gave it.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="b-body">Measured on the 52 cameras whose spectral sensitivities rawtoaces-data publishes, through PIXL's fitted profiles and the 190 training reflectances: under D65 every patch lies inside; under D50 one deep-red patch lies outside on 15 cameras (by at most 5.0e-3 in xy); under tungsten the same patch on all 52 (1.8e-2); under LED and fluorescent light that patch and a few beside it (3.6e-2).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap band" id="matrices" aria-labelledby="mat-title">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="head-stack">
        <span class="b-micro">Matrices</span>
        <h2 class="b-disp h2" id="mat-title">Computed from the primaries.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="b-body">Computed from the primaries and white above in exact arithmetic and rounded to ten decimals. They are informative: where they and the chromaticities disagree in the last place, the chromaticities are right.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="matrices">
      {
        matrices.map((x) => (
          <figure class="matrix">
            <figcaption class="b-micro">{x.title}</figcaption>
            <table class="b-mono">
              <tbody>{x.m.map((r) => <tr>{r.map((c) => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
            </table>
          </figure>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="b-body"><strong>Luminance</strong> is the Y row: Y = 0.3377437773 R + 0.6753876729 G − 0.0131314503 B. Blue's weight is negative. A colour's luminance is never below zero; a value outside the visible range can have a negative one.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap band spec" id="profiles" aria-labelledby="icc-title">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="head-stack">
        <span class="b-micro">ICC profiles</span>
        <h2 class="b-disp h2" id="icc-title">Two profiles. Download them.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="b-body">ICC version 4 matrix/TRC display profiles, written by PIXL's colour engine (Little CMS) and held to the specification by PIXL's tests.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="downloads">
      <li>
        <span class="b-micro">Integer form</span>
        <h3 class="b-disp">PixlRGB.icc</h3>
        <p class="b-body">For standard-dynamic-range files at 10 bits or more. A pure power curve with γ = 2.2 as an ICC profile holds it.</p>
        <a class="b-cta sec" href={profile('PixlRGB.icc')} download>Download PixlRGB.icc</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="b-micro">Linear form</span>
        <h3 class="b-disp">PixlRGB-linear.icc</h3>
        <p class="b-body">For linear floats, including HDR masters with light above white. A PIXL file in linear PixlRGB carries this one.</p>
        <a class="b-cta sec" href={profile('PixlRGB-linear.icc')} download>Download PixlRGB-linear.icc</a>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="b-body">Their description tags read “PixlRGB” and “linear PixlRGB”, and their copyright tags state this licence and credit the CIE. The connection space is D50, with the Bradford adaptation from D65 stated in the chad tag, as ICC v4 asks. They carry no chrm tag: its numbers cannot be negative. No CICP code point names PixlRGB.</p>
    <p class="b-body">The integer form is computed with the numbers its profile states (γ = 144179/65536 and colorants in s15.16 in the D50 connection space), so that a reader working from the profile and one working from this page land on the same samples. The linear form is the matrices above exactly.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap band spec" id="in-pixl" aria-labelledby="in-title">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="head-stack">
        <span class="b-micro">In PIXL</span>
        <h2 class="b-disp h2" id="in-title">Every float stage works in it.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="b-body">Outputs leave it through PIXL's conversion into their own space.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="b-body">Outputs leave PixlRGB through PIXL's conversion into their own space: Display P3 for standard pictures, Rec.2020 for PQ and HLG, which are defined in Rec.2020's primaries. An HDR master written as floats stays in linear PixlRGB, described by the linear profile. A caller can also name PixlRGB as an output space, in its integer form (written at 10 bits or more) or linear.</p>
    <p class="b-body">In the master, a grade step that pushes a colour out of the visible range has it pulled back onto the range's edge with its luminance and dominant wavelength kept; values that were outside before the step are left alone.</p>
    <p class="b-body">It is the working space of <a class="textlink" href={href('playroom', '/#colour', 'engine')}>Pixl Playroom</a>, whose Scopes draw a CIE 1976 chart with PixlRGB and the reference gamuts over your photo.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap band spec" id="licence" aria-labelledby="lic-title">
    <h2 class="b-disp h2" id="lic-title">Licence and credits</h2>
    <p class="b-body">PixlRGB version 1, 2026-10-03. Copyright © 2026 Syed Ali (xuckless), PixlFoundation. This specification and the ICC profiles beside it are licensed under the <a class="textlink" href={licence} rel="license noopener" target="_blank">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International licence (CC BY-SA 4.0)</a>. This page reproduces the specification from PIXL Engine's source, with the chart and download links added.</p>
    <p class="b-body">The primaries are derived from the CIE 1931 standard colorimetric observer: CIE 2019, <em>Colour-matching functions of CIE 1931 standard colorimetric observer</em>, International Commission on Illumination (CIE), Vienna, DOI 10.25039/CIE.DS.xvudnb9b, licensed under CC BY 4.0. The CIE's data points are used as measured facts; no CIE table is reproduced here. The chart's spectral locus is drawn from the standard published chromaticities of that observer.</p>
    <p class="b-body">The camera measurements above use rawtoaces-data (Academy Software Foundation), commit e9b8503, licensed under the Apache License 2.0.</p>
    <p class="b-body">Questions, or want PixlRGB in your own software? <a class="textlink" href={contact}>hello@pixlfoundation.com</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
